      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cherry Point earned its name from a local post office nestled among cherry trees, later becoming Marine Corps Air Station Cherry Point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Cherry_Point",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "On May 20, 1942, the air station was commissioned as Cunningham Field, honoring Marine Corps aviator Lt. Col. Alfred A.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Cherry_Point",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cherry Point’s primary World War II mission was to train Marines for the Pacific theater.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Cherry_Point",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Its runway system made Cherry Point an alternate emergency landing site for Space Shuttle launches from Cape Canaveral.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Corps_Air_Station_Cherry_Point",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During 1943, Cherry Point hosted Army and Navy squadrons that sank a German U‑boat off North Carolina’s coast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
